# Local-chain deployment artifacts are ephemeral + deployer-specific — regenerate with
# `npm run deploy:local`. Only real-network deployments (Amoy/mainnet/…) are committed.
packages/sdk/src/deployments/31337.json
packages/sdk/src/deployments/1337.json

# ⚠️ The generated SDK surface — `packages/sdk/src/generated/` and
# `packages/sdk/src/deployments/deployments.gen.ts` — is COMMITTED, and used not to be.
#
# It was ignored so that a dev's local anvil deployment could not leak a baked address into the
# package, which is a real risk: codegen reads every `src/deployments/*.json`, including the
# 31337/1337 files ignored just above, and bakes the addresses into `react.ts`.
#
# That risk is now handled by the `Committed codegen is up to date` step in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`,
# which regenerates on a clean checkout (no local deployment files) and fails on any diff. A leaked
# local address cannot be merged; before, it simply could not be seen.
#
# Two things were bought by committing it, and neither was reachable while it was ignored:
#   1. 🔴 release-please attributes a commit to a package by the PATHS IT TOUCHES, and the only
#      package is rooted at `packages/sdk`. A contracts-only change therefore cut NO release, while
#      still changing the ABI that the next unrelated release would publish. AO-713 landed a
#      breaking `settlePrimary` selector and `INTENT_TYPEHASH` this way: had a `fix(sdk):` commit
#      followed it, the breaking ABI would have shipped as a PATCH to consumers on carets.
#   2. An ABI change is now REVIEWABLE. The typehash and selector change in AO-713 was invisible in
#      its own pull request, because the only artefact carrying it was generated at publish time.

